Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

REQ-OPS-MILESTONE-LEDGER-6262. north-star.md §1 already defines churn -- "a
milestone that produces a new version of an existing artifact without moving the
headline is noise" -- and nothing computed it.

First measurement, milestones .540-.549, 108 tasks: 34 BLOCKED (31%), 31
scaffolding (29%), 17 null (16%), 16 substantive (15%), 10 unclassified or
missing. reproducible_total_levels 183 and games 25 unchanged across all ten.

The biggest category is BLOCKED, and that is the actionable finding: nearly a
third of tasks never ran -- validation commands timed out, accepted_count=0,
readiness controls did not pass, gate_check_failed. Three milestones produced zero
substantive artifacts. That is a process problem, not a research problem, and the
cheapest thing here to fix.

Deliberately NOT a gate. Scaffolding, blocked tasks and honest nulls are all
legitimate work, and gating on the share would reward relabelling verdicts --
exactly the failure verdict_row_consistency_lint exists to catch. It reports; a
human judges.

The classifier is deliberately ungenerous and was corrected once mid-build:
milestone-transition tasks were landing in "substantive" because their verdicts
read like results while measuring nothing, and reclassifying them dropped the
share from 19% to 15%. An unrecognised verdict counts as UNCLASSIFIED, never as
substantive, because a visibility tool that drifts generous becomes reassurance. 7
tests pin that. One test also had to be corrected: it quoted a truncated verdict
and failed because the marker that catches it lived in the clause that was cut --
assert against real strings, not remembered ones.

Caveat recorded with the number: it counts artifacts by verdict shape, not value.
One measurement that closes a real question beats five that do not. It is a prompt
to ask whether the headline moved, not a target to optimise.

